\begin_body

\begin_layout Section

\lang brazilian
The impact of the wealth-to-salary ratio on economic inequality
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:introduction"

\end_inset

 Economic activity can be interpreted through the lens of complex systems,
 in which collective outcomes arise from interactions among heterogeneous agents
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "JUSUP20221,yakovenko2009,Farmer2009"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Particularly within econophysics,
 this perspective has proven particularly useful for understanding robust empirical patterns,
 especially those associated with the distributions of income and wealth
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "yakovenko2009,shaikh"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 In this context,
 a range of agent based models (ABM),
 including random-asset exchange models
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "chatterjee2007econophysics,repec:spr:eurphb:v:73:y:2010:i:1:p:145-153"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 have been developed in the literature of econophysics.
 Among them,
 the Social Architecture of Capitalism (SA) model introduced by Ian Wright
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Wright_2005"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 provides a simple yet empirically grounded description of interactions between firms and workers,
 successfully capturing key stylized facts of wealth distributions,
 with a clear two-regime structure
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "dragulescu2001,yakovenko2023"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 In recent works
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Borba2025,Borba2026"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 we conducted a systematic exploration of the parameter space of the SA model and we identified a parameter governing the separation between the two regimes.
 This parameter,
 denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

,
 is defined as the ratio of average wealth per capita to the mean wage.
 Our analysis shows that,
 in this model,
 the overall shape of the wealth distribution—
including the emergence of a gap between classes,
 increasing concentration of wealth,
 and the disappearance of an intermediate stratum at high values of 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 are largely determined by this ratio,
 with only minor sensitivity to system size.
 In particular,
 inequality,
 as measured by the Gini coefficient
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "PLATAPEREZ201579"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 increases monotonically with 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

.
 Moreover these results are robust holding for realistic modifications of the original proposal
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Borba2026"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Motivated by these ABM results,
 we conduct an empirical assessment to examine whether the theoretical predictions are supported by real-world data.
 To this end,
 we construct empirical estimates of 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

,
 using measures of average employee compensation and net personal wealth,
 drawing —
among other sources—
 on data from the World Inequality Database (WID)
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "WID"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 We find a positive relationship between 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 and standard measures of inequality for mature and advanced economies especially during periods known for the prevalence of liberal policies and stability,
 consistent with the model’s predictions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Obtaining reliable data on income and wealth remains a significant challenge.
 Administrative regions often differ in how they define and measure these variables,
 and various organizations may employ distinct methodologies.
 Moreover,
 even within the same institution,
 methodological changes can occur over time.
 As a result,
 data are scarce for certain regions,
 and cross-comparisons between datasets compiled by different organizations are often nontrivial.
 These limitations are taken into account in the analysis and comparisons that follow.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Analysis of estimates of 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 from real data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:real"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We start by analyzing,
 in Section
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:single"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

,
 the temporal evolution of the relationship between 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 and the Gini coefficient within individual countries,
 focusing on the United States,
 France,
 and the United Kingdom.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Section
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:groups"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

,
 we extend the analysis from individual countries to a comparative study of groups of advanced economies using two different data sources.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Individual countries
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:single"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the following analyses,
 unless otherwise stated,
 inequality is measured by the Gini coefficient of pre-tax national income
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
According to the WID definition,
 national income aims to measure the total income available to the residents of a given country
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "WIDglossario"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and the Gini index of net personal wealth
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
According to the WID definition,
 net personal wealth corresponds to the total value of non-financial and financial assets held by households,
 minus their debts.
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 To estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

,
 we proxy 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{p}$
\end_inset

 by average employee compensation
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
According to the WID definition,
 employee compensation corresponds to the total remuneration,
 in cash or in kind,
 paid by employers to employees in return for work done.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and we proxy 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{w}$
\end_inset

 by net personal wealth,
 both expressed per adult individual and in local currency.
 All data are obtained from the World Inequality Database (WID)
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "WID"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
%%% 
\backslash
subsubsection{United States}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We first consider the United States (U.S.).
 Figure
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:R-Real"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 shows the evolution of inequality in the U.S.
 from 1984 to 2021.
 A clear positive relationship,
 as predicted by the model,
 is observed:
 increases in 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 are associated with higher Gini coefficients for both income and wealth.
 Using Spearman’s rank correlation coefficient,
 we find a strong and statistically significant correlation between 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 and income inequality (
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=0.95$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $p<0.05$
\end_inset

).
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Throughout the remainder of the text,
 all statistical analyses are assessed for statistical significance using a significance threshold of 
\begin_inset Formula $p\leq0.05$
\end_inset

.
 For Spearman's rank correlation coefficient,
 statistical significance is evaluated using a permutation test.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

