\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
OECD countries:
 Income Gini index versus the 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 factor,
 using the most recent data available within the period 2015-2019.
 Group A is shown with filled circles,
 and Group B with open circles.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:R-OECD"

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So when we focus on more advanced and long-established economies,
 we observe a stronger and more statistically significant correlation between 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 and inequality,
 as measured by the Gini coefficient.
 One way to interpret this result is through Erik Olin Wright’s conception of capitalism.
 According to Wright,
 to describe an economy as capitalist means that capitalist relations are the dominant relations within a given economic ecosystem,
 but not necessarily the only relations present in that economy
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "wright2019anticapitalista"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since the SA model represents a purely capitalist system,
 we may hypothesize that it better describes economies in which capitalist relations are more systematically institutionalized across social and economic life.
 This may explain why the model appears to fit more advanced and long-established capitalist economies more closely.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The SA model also describes a closed economy.
 In a globalized world,
 however,
 treating a country as a closed system may appear inconsistent with the realities of international economic integration.
 Ellen Meiksins Wood argues that national capitalist economies are interconnected and embedded in a global system governed by general capitalist laws,
 which bind capitalist economies together within a broader world economy
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "wood"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thomas Piketty shows that the net foreign wealth positions of most advanced economies have remained relatively small,
 often close to zero or slightly negative
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "piketty2014capital"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This suggests that external imbalances may not dominate domestic wealth dynamics in advanced economies,
 although non-advanced economies may behave differently.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To further investigate the role of international integration,
 we compute trade openness,
 defined as trade as a percentage of GDP.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
World Bank definition:
 Trade is the sum of exports and imports of goods and services.
 This indicator is expressed as a percentage of Gross Domestic Product (GDP),
 which is the total income earned through the production of goods and services in an economic territory during an accounting period.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using World Bank data for the years analyzed
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "WB"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 when ranking the countries in the sample by trade openness,
 six of the nine most open economies belong to Group B,
 representing 75% of that group.
 This suggests an association between greater trade openness and a more recent or less consolidated status as an advanced economy within our sample.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The two exceptions within Group B are Chile and Poland,
 which are still not classified as advanced economies.
 To put it another way,
 among the 9 countries with higher trade openness,
 the only 3 that belong to group A,
 are Ireland,
 Luxembourg,
 and the Netherlands.
 These are also the only countries in the sample that appear among the top 10 in 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

The world's biggest enablers of corporate tax abuse
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "tax"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 an index of corporate tax havens.
 This may introduce statistical distortions,
 since their trade-to-GDP ratios can be inflated by multinational profit-shifting and accounting practices.
 If these three countries are also excluded,
 the Spearman's rank coefficient increases to 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=0.90$
\end_inset

.
 The complete ranking is shown in Table 
