 which are marked with a cross (
\begin_inset Formula $\dagger$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "table:all2"

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus,
 countries with more open economies tend either to have a shorter history as advanced economies or to exhibit distortions associated with their role as tax havens.
 Taken together,
 these results suggest that a closed-economy model such as SA,
 despite its limitations,
 may approximate some structural features of large,
 mature capitalist economies that are less affected by extreme openness or accounting distortions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
WID data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Panel data analysis is a standard tool in econometrics and therefore provides a natural approach for testing the relationship between the parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 and inequality,
 as measured by the Gini coefficient,
 in empirical data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We construct a balanced panel dataset in which all countries are observed in every year between 1995 and 2019.
 The end date is chosen to avoid the effects of the COVID-19 pandemic.
 The data are obtained from the WID,
 for countries classified as advanced economies by the IMF since 1995
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "imf1995weo"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Of the 23 countries listed as advanced economies,
 or industrialized economies in the terminology used at the time,
 Iceland was excluded due to lack of data.
 In addition to the 19 countries listed in Table
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "table:all"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

,
 the other three countries analyzed are Austria,
 Luxembourg,
 and Spain.
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since the original definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 in the SA model is based on averages computed over the entire population,
 the construction of a global indicator should account for differences in country population sizes.
 Otherwise,
 small economies would contribute with the same weight as highly populated countries,
 masking the differences in the relative size of national economies within the global economy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For this reason,
 we calculate averages weighted by the employed population across countries 
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
WID definition:
 Number of employed individuals.
\end_layout

\end_inset

.The average of national Gini coefficients is not mathematically equivalent to the Gini coefficient that would be obtained by merging all national populations into a single global distribution.
 Nevertheless,
 due to the difficulty in obtaining data for calculating the aggregate data,
 this represents a first attempt to account for population differences among countries.
 To keep the approach consistent,
 the average value of 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 is calculated in the same way.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Taking yearly averages across all countries in the sample,
 we obtain Spearman correlation coefficients of 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=0.73$
\end_inset

 for wealth inequality and 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=0.95$
\end_inset

 for income inequality.
 The final result is shown in Fig.
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:22countries"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

