\begin_layout Standard
Just as we discussed in the case of the U.S.,
 we can see that the data for the decade following the Great Recession deviates from the trend line.
 What the analysis of this data indicates is the existence of a tendency for an increase in the ratio 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 to be accompanied by an increase in inequality (both income and wealth) as measured by the Gini coefficient.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Before proceeding,
 it is also worth making a few comments about the individual countries included in the dataset.
 Applying Spearman's rank correlation test individually to each country to examine the relationship between the Gini coefficient and 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 for the 22 countries in the sample.
 Excluding results that were not statistically significant,
 
\begin_inset Formula $82\%$
\end_inset

 of the countries showed a positive correlation between 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

