\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Ranking of the analyzed countries according to GDP (in thousands) in current prices.
 Countries with mixed correlation coefficients are indicated by an asterisk,
 and countries with only a negative sign are indicated by a minus sign.
 Countries that presented only positive coefficients do not receive any marking.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "table:all"

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The next step is to analyze the group of countries as a whole.
 To make proper use of panel data,
 before applying the Ordinary Least Squares regression technique we subtract the mean of each time series in order to reposition the distribution of points of all countries around the origin.
 In practice,
 this transformation removes the average level of each country and retains only the fluctuations around its own historical mean,
 corresponding to the econometric technique known as Fixed Effects.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In addition,
 it is interesting to take into account the distribution of the employed population across different countries obtained from WID.
 In order to use population as a weight,
 Stata software requires a constant weight for each country.
 Therefore,
 we used the average population of each country calculated over the 25-year period analyzed.
 It should be noted that the ratio between the populations of the countries changed little during this period.
 The country with the greatest absolute variation was the U.S.,
 the proportion of its population relative to the total population of the countries analyzed varied by less than 3%,
 with maximum and minimum values of 33.81% and 30.89%,
 respectively.
 The same qualitative results were obtained when using any particular year as a reference,
 so we consider the average to be a good approximation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore,
 the contribution of each country to the minimization of residuals was weighted according to the fraction of workers represented by that country within the total working population of the sample.
 This corresponds to a weighted Fixed Effects analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We choose this procedure,
 motivated by the objective of establishing a parsimonious baseline specification.
 Given the wide range of possible panel data approaches,
 we begin with a simpler framework.
 This baseline serves as a benchmark against which more demanding specifications can be evaluated.
 Specifically,
 it allows for a transparent evaluation of the relationship between 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 and inequality before introducing additional layers of complexity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We estimate a positive regression coefficient for both the income and wealth Gini coefficients.
 The coefficient is 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta=8\times10^{-3}$
\end_inset

 for wealth and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta=9\times10^{-3}$
\end_inset

 for income,
 which corresponds to a change of near 
\begin_inset Formula $~0.01$
\end_inset

 of the Gini coefficient for each unit increase in 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

.
 Given the relatively low temporal variability of Gini coefficients,
 even modest estimated effects may correspond to economically meaningful changes.
 For example,
 when examining the variation in the global Gini coefficient for income (Purchasing Power parity - PPP) between 1980 and 2024,
 the maximum and minimum values are 0.86 and 0.83,
 respectively (if measured in Market Exchange Rates - MER,
 the values are 0.90 and 0.88).
 This corresponds to a maximum variation of only 0.03 (0.02) points over more than 40 years.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Wealth-to-income ratio
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To the best of our knowledge,
 no studies in the economics literature investigate precisely the relationship between inequality and 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

.
 However,
 several works may contribute to the discussion.
 In Piketty’s terminology,
 the closest related quantity is 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

,
 the wealth-to-income ratio,
 rather than the wealth-to-wage ratio represented here by 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

.
 In a study by Piketty and Zucman,
 eight advanced economies are analyzed,
 and the authors concluded that in all these countries there has been a gradual increase in the wealth-income ratio in recent decades.
 According to the authors,
 this increase is not necessarily problematic in itself,
 but it raises important questions concerning taxation and regulation,
 since high wealth-to-income ratios imply that wealth inequality is likely to play a central role in the overall structure of inequality in the twenty-first century
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "repec:oup:qjecon:v:129:y:2014:i:3:p:1255-1310"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
%https://gabriel-zucman.eu/files/PikettyZucman2014QJE.pdf
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Madsen,
 on the other hand,
 focuses on the last eight centuries of British history and engages in critical dialogue with Piketty's work,
 but also concludes that the data suggest that variations in income inequality are accompanied by variations in 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

,
 i.e.,
 the time series for income inequality follows a pattern very similar to that of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 The author further suggests that the wealth-to-income ratio may serve as a proxy for inequality 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "MADSEN2019246"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given the documented relationship between inequality and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

,
 it is useful to consider the relationship between 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

,
 or,
 more specifically,
 between average income per capita and mean wages.
 Although an increase in national income does not necessarily translate into an equivalent rise in mean wages,
 there is still a structural relationship between the two.
 In aggregate terms,
 wages constitute a component of national income,
 therefore,
 an expansion in national income may be expected to be associated with an increase in the average level of wages
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Przekota2023CausalityIT"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus,
 one may expect 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 to exhibit a qualitative long-run tendency,
 insofar as average wages tend to increase with national income.
 In other words,
 an increase in one tends to be accompanied by an increase in the other,
 albeit with different magnitudes,
 mediated by changes in the labor share ( the share of national income paid to workers as compensation),
 which determines how closely compensation dynamics track income growth.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Denoting the labor share by 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 and national income by 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

,
 a first approximation for the mean wage is 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{p}=sY/E$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

 is the employed population.
 Defining the employment ratio as 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa=E/N$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is the total population,
 and assuming that all labor compensation takes the form of wages,
 the average wealth is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{w}=W/N$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

 denotes total wealth.
 Under these assumptions,
 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 can be written as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
R=\frac{\overline{w}}{\overline{p}}=\frac{W/N}{sY/E}=\frac{1}{s}\frac{W}{Y}\frac{E}{N}=\frac{\kappa}{s}\beta.
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In other words,
 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 increases with 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 when the labor share (
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

) and the employment ratio (
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa$
\end_inset

) remain constant.
 Holding all other variables fixed,
 an increase in 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa$
\end_inset

 also raises 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

.
 This is because keeping the total wage bill (
\begin_inset Formula $sY$
\end_inset

) constant while increasing the number of employed workers reduces the mean wage.
 As a result,
 the relationship between 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

,
 unemployment,
 and inequality is nontrivial and may be non-monotonic,
 as explored in our recent work
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Borba2026"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 where we show that,
 according to the model,
 starting from a scenario of extremely high unemployment,
 increases in 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 initially reduce inequality as both the mean wage and the unemployment rate decline.
 However,
 once unemployment reaches more realistic levels,
 the expected relationship emerges,
 with inequality increasing as 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 continues to grow.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Data on the U.S.
 employment-population ratio over the past two decades (2006–2026) indicate remarkable stability throughout most of the period.
 Even during the 2008 financial crisis,
 it remained within the range of 58.3% to 63.3%.
 The main exception was the COVID-19 pandemic,
 when the ratio fell to 51.2% in April 2020 before recovering to above 58.3% by July 2021
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "taxa"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Regarding the labor share,
 empirical studies suggest that this ratio has remained within a relatively narrow range,
 typically between 0.5 and 0.6,
 over long historical periods.
 This regularity is a well-known stylized fact in economics,
 often referred to as Bowley's law
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "farjoun2022labor"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Analyzing a shorter time window,
 Karabarbounis and Neiman show that there has been a significant decline in labor share since the early 1980s,
 occurring mainly within the large majority of countries and industries
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "repec:nbr:nberwo:19136"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 The Economic Policy Institute (EPI),
 analyzing the case of the United States,
 found positive annual growth in both productivity
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
According to the Economic Policy Institute,
 productivity measures how much income is generated,
 on average,
 in an hour of work.
 As productivity grows,
 each hour of work generates more income over time.
 If productivity grows at a faster rate than compensation,
 this implies that a decreasing fraction of the income generated by labor is paid to workers as compensation,
 which is likely to reduce the labor share.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and compensation (wages and benefits) paid per hour to workers,
 even though between 1979 and 2025 productivity grew at an annual rate of 1.6% while compensation grew by only 0.6%
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "epi_productivity_pay_gap_2026"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 widening the inequality
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
There are some challenges to these findings,
 such as the debate raised by the conservative think tank American Enterprise Institute (AEI) regarding the methodological choices adopted by the EPI.
 However,
 what the institute argues is that compensation growth has kept pace with productivity growth,
 not that it has outpaced it.
 Therefore,
 this does not undermine our analysis
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "AEI"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However,
 this does not contradict our findings.
 Positive growth in 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is still related to positive growth in 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

.
 Moreover,
 differences in growth rates arising from changes in the labor share over recent decades may have contributed to increasing inequality and are reflected in the behavior of 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 our analysis of the parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 finds indirect support in the existing literature.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Final remarks and conclusion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:final"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The results for the three countries analyzed here,
 together with those from the subsequent cross-country analysis,
 suggest that the model better captures the dynamics of advanced capitalist economies that are institutionally stable and economically mature.
 Deviations from the main trend appear to be associated with atypical economic periods,
 such as crises,
 stronger dependence on international markets,
 or lower levels of economic development.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An interesting direction for future research would be to test whether these patterns hold at the global level.
 When considering the world economy as a whole,
 it must necessarily be treated as a closed system.
 However,
 this task faces the challenge of obtaining reliable data that accurately reflect the state of the global economy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To conclude,
 it is useful to return to a classical insight that remains directly relevant to the present analysis.
 As Adam Smith wrote in The Wealth of Nations:
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

No society can surely be flourishing and happy,
 of which the far greater part of the members are poor and miserable.
 It is but equity,
 besides,
 that they who feed,
 clothe,
 and lodge the whole body of the people,
 should have such a share of the produce of their own labor as to be themselves tolerably well fed,
 clothed,
 and lodged.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


